{
 "id": "20-09-program-the-lut",
 "title": "Program the LUT as a majority vote",
 "kind": "logic",
 "story": "This is one 3-input lookup table (LUT), the basic cell of an FPGA. A tree of 2:1 multiplexers uses the inputs a, b and c to pick one of eight configuration bits: row a b c = 0 0 0 picks bit 0, row 0 0 1 picks bit 1, and so on up to row 1 1 1, which picks bit 7. Each configuration slot is fed from a constant 1: a 'buf' gate passes the 1 through (the bit is 1), a 'not' gate turns it into 0. Program the LUT so that its output is 1 when at least two of the three inputs are 1 (a majority vote, as used to combine three redundant sensors).",
